# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## What this repo is

Robot Framework test suite for SK Telecom **PG (Policy Gateway)** integration, covering three interfaces: **NAG**, **PCF**, and **LRS**. Each interface speaks the same 8-octet binary header but differs in role (client vs. server), body encoding (JSON vs. fixed-length ASCII), and message types. Documentation, comments, test names, and log messages are in Korean — preserve that style when editing.

## Common commands

```bash
bash run_tests.sh nag                       # NAG suite (client → PG:8012)
bash run_tests.sh pcf                       # PCF suite (client → PG:8011, also opens NAG session first)
bash run_tests.sh lrs                       # LRS suite (server, listens on :8890 for LRS(PG))
bash run_tests.sh all                       # all suites
bash run_tests.sh smoke                     # only --include smoke
bash run_tests.sh nag --log-msg             # set PG_LOG_MSG=1 for REQ/RESP tracing
bash run_tests.sh all 192.168.1.1           # override NAG_PG_HOST / PCF_PG_HOST

# Single test (bypasses run_tests.sh)
python -m robot --test "TC-NAG-010*" tests/nag/
python -m robot --test "TC-LRS-006*" tests/lrs/

# Tag filtering
python -m robot --include negative tests/
python -m robot --include lte tests/
```

Output goes to `results/<YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS>/` (`report.html`, `log.html`).

## Architecture

### Three interfaces, one shared protocol header

`resources/TcpHelper.py` is the only Python module — it implements an 8-octet big-endian header (`build_header`/`parse_header`) used by all three interfaces. The first byte distinguishes interface family:

- `0x00` — NAG/PCF (ProtoVer=0), JSON body
- `0x20` — LRS (ProtoVer=01'B), fixed-length ASCII body packed via `pack_fields` / `unpack_fields`

`txn_id` must never be 0 (regulatory requirement; `Next TXN ID` keyword enforces this).

### Connection direction matters

- **NAG / PCF**: tool acts as **client**, connects to PG. Use `tcp_connect` / `send_message` / `receive_message` (JSON).
- **LRS**: tool acts as **server**, LRS(PG) connects in. Use `server_start` / `server_accept` / `send_lrs_message` / `receive_lrs_message` (fixed-length ASCII).

The PCF suite is special: its `Suite Setup` (`Suite Connect With NAG`) opens **both** the NAG socket (8012, for session pre-registration) **and** the PCF socket (8011) before any PCF test runs. Both sockets must stay open for the whole suite or `Check PCF And NAG Socket` triggers `Fatal Error`.

### Suite-level socket sharing

Sockets are **opened once per suite** and shared across all test cases via Suite Variables (`${NAG_SOCK}`, `${PCF_SOCK}`, `${LRS_CONN}`, `${LRS_SRV_SOCK}`). Each `Test Setup` calls a `Check ... Socket` keyword that escalates a closed socket to **`Fatal Error`** — Robot Framework then aborts the entire suite, since subsequent tests cannot run without the connection. Do not add per-test connect/disconnect logic.

For LRS, `Suite LRS Accept` blocks on `Server Accept` waiting for LRS(PG) to dial in (timeout `${LRS_ACCEPT_TIMEOUT}` = 30s). When running the LRS suite, the operator must trigger LRS(PG) to connect within that window.

### Layer map

```
tests/<iface>/<iface>_tests.robot     ← test cases (TC-NAG-xxx, TC-PCF-xxx, TC-LRS-xxx)
        │ uses
        ▼
resources/common_keywords.robot       ← Robot keywords: Suite setup/teardown, send/recv, validations
        │ uses (WITH NAME Tcp)
        ▼
resources/TcpHelper.py                ← raw socket + header pack/unpack
```

`resources/variables.robot` centralizes hosts/ports, message-type constants (`${MSG_HELLO_REQ}`, etc.), response codes (`${CODE_*}`, `${LRS_CODE_*}`), and test data (MDNs, APNs, TIDs). Edit values here rather than hardcoding in tests. Many `${LRS_*}` variables are flagged `# TODO: 실환경 ...` and need replacement with real-environment values before LRS suites pass against production PG.

### Message-type constant collisions are intentional

`${MSG_PCF_ZONE_REQ}` and `${MSG_LOC_INFO_REQ}` are both `0x05`; `${MSG_PCF_ZONE_RESP}` and `${MSG_LOC_INFO_RESP}` are both `0x06`. The same opcode means different things on PCF vs. LRS sockets. Use the interface-specific constant name to make intent clear.

### Tags used by --include

`smoke`, `negative`, `validation`, `lte`, `5g`, plus per-interface (`nag`, `pcf`, `lrs`) and per-feature (`hello`, `ping`, `subs-zone`, `subs-cellid`, `location`, `adot`).
